  ["Combinatorics","Core","Pigeonhole Principle","More objects than boxes forces a collision.","If n+1 objects occupy n boxes, at least one box has two objects.","Use counts to detect collisions in finite algorithms.","The theorem gives a guarantee; code implements a finite counting situation.","pigeonhole"],
  ["Combinatorics","Core","Inclusion-Exclusion","Count unions by alternating intersections.","|A∪B|=|A|+|B|-|A∩B| and generalizes to many sets.","Represent finite sets and compute intersections or iterate subsets.","The number of inclusion-exclusion terms grows exponentially with set count.","inclusion-exclusion"],
  ["Combinatorics","Core","Binomial Coefficients","Counts selections and coefficients in expansions.","C(n,k) counts k-subsets of an n-set.","Use dynamic programming or multiplicative computation.","Exact integer growth can exceed standard numeric ranges.","binomial coefficient"],
  ["Combinatorics","Advanced","Generating Functions","Encode sequences as formal power series.","Coefficients represent sequence values and algebraic operations transform sequences.","Represent truncated coefficient arrays and perform convolution.","Formal infinite series become finite truncations in code.","generating functions"],
  ["Combinatorics","Advanced","Integer Partitions","Ways to write an integer as a sum of positive integers ignoring order.","Partition functions count decompositions of n.","Use dynamic programming over target and smallest allowed part.","The mathematical count may be exact; DP computes finite states with chosen recurrence.","integer partitions"],

  ["Probability","Core","Sample Spaces","Set of possible outcomes of a random experiment.","A sample space contains outcomes and events are subsets.","Represent finite outcomes as arrays/Sets or generate them algorithmically.","A finite list can represent a finite model; continuous spaces require mathematical density models rather than enumeration.","sample space|outcomes"],
  ["Probability","Core","Conditional Probability","Probability of an event given another event.","P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B) when P(B)>0.","Compute joint and conditional frequencies/probabilities from a model or dataset.","Estimated probabilities from data are not automatically the true model probabilities.","conditional probability"],
  ["Probability","Core","Bayes' Theorem","Reverse conditional probabilities using prior and likelihood.","P(A|B)=P(B|A)P(A)/P(B).","Implement weighted probability calculations and normalization.","The code is only as meaningful as the chosen priors, likelihoods and evidence model.","Bayes theorem"],
  ["Probability","Core","Random Variables","Variables mapping outcomes to numerical values.","A random variable assigns numbers to outcomes and has a distribution.","Represent samples or probability tables; generate observations with RNGs.","Randomness in code is algorithmic and finite; probability theory describes a model/distribution.","random variable"],
  ["Probability","Core","Expected Value","Probability-weighted average of outcomes.","E[X]=Σxp(x) for discrete variables.","Reduce over values and probabilities or estimate with sample means.","A sample average is an estimator and converges under conditions; it is not the expectation itself.","expectation|mean"],
  ["Probability","Core","Variance & Standard Deviation","Measures of spread around the mean.","Var(X)=E[(X-E[X])²].","Compute stable variance using algorithms such as Welford's method.","Different computational formulas have different numerical stability properties.","variance|standard deviation"],
  ["Probability","Core","Binomial Distribution","Number of successes in n independent Bernoulli trials.","P(X=k)=C(n,k)p^k(1-p)^(n-k).","Compute probabilities or sample with a binomial RNG algorithm.","Tiny probabilities and large n require stable log/probability methods.","binomial distribution"],
  ["Probability","Core","Normal Distribution","Continuous bell-shaped distribution parameterized by mean and variance.","Density and CDF describe Gaussian probabilities.","Evaluate density with Math.exp or use numerical CDF methods.","A continuous distribution is a mathematical model; code evaluates approximations and samples.","normal distribution|Gaussian"],
  ["Probability","Advanced","Markov Chains","State transitions depending only on current state.","A Markov chain is defined by a transition matrix or kernel.","Represent transition matrices and repeatedly multiply probability vectors or simulate paths.","Finite simulation approximates long-run behavior; matrix calculations describe the model exactly.","Markov chain|transition matrix"],
  ["Probability","Advanced","Monte Carlo Methods","Estimate quantities using random sampling.","Expected values and integrals can be approximated from samples.","Generate many samples and average or count outcomes.","The estimate has sampling error; more samples reduce error statistically rather than guaranteeing exactness.","Monte Carlo|simulation"],

  ["Statistics","Core","Mean","Arithmetic average.","Mean is sum divided by count for a dataset.","Use reduce and length, preferably with stable accumulation for large data.","The sample mean is a statistic; numerical summation can accumulate rounding error.","mean|average"],
  ["Statistics","Core","Median","Middle order statistic.","Median is the middle value after sorting, with conventions for even sample sizes.","Sort a copy and select the middle index(es).","Sorting is algorithmic and changes complexity; the mathematical median depends on the dataset.","median"],
  ["Statistics","Core","Mode","Most frequent value or values.","Mode identifies maximal frequency.","Use a Map frequency table.","Ties can produce multiple modes; code must choose or return all tied values.","mode|frequency"],
  ["Statistics","Core","Variance","Average squared deviation from a mean under a chosen denominator convention.","Population and sample variance use different denominators.","Implement explicitly with n or n-1 and document the convention.","The mathematical definition depends on context; code must not silently choose the wrong estimator.","variance|sample variance"],
  ["Statistics","Core","Correlation","Standardized measure of linear association.","Pearson correlation divides covariance by standard deviations.","Compute centered products and normalize.","Correlation measures association under assumptions; code cannot infer causation from the number.","correlation|Pearson"],
  ["Statistics","Core","Covariance","Joint variation between variables.","Covariance measures whether variables tend to deviate together.","Compute paired deviations and average under a stated convention.","Scaling changes covariance; numerical centering affects stability.","covariance"],
  ["Statistics","Core","Linear Regression","Fit a line minimizing squared residuals.","Ordinary least squares chooses coefficients minimizing squared error.","Solve via stable linear algebra or formulas for simple regression.","A fitted line is a model estimated from data; its meaning depends on assumptions and sampling.","regression|least squares"],
  ["Statistics","Advanced","Confidence Intervals","Intervals produced by a specified statistical procedure with stated coverage properties.","A confidence procedure has repeated-sampling interpretation under assumptions.","Compute interval endpoints using estimates, standard errors and critical values.","An interval's interpretation depends on the method and assumptions; code must implement the correct procedure.","confidence interval"],
  ["Statistics","Advanced","Hypothesis Testing","Null models, test statistics, rejection rules and p-values.","A test evaluates data relative to a specified null hypothesis.","Compute a test statistic and compare with a threshold or p-value.","A p-value is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true; code must preserve the statistical definition.","hypothesis testing|p-value"],
  ["Statistics","Advanced","Maximum Likelihood","Choose parameters maximizing likelihood of observed data.","MLE optimizes the probability model's likelihood over parameters.","Implement objective functions and numerical optimization.","The mathematical estimator depends on the model; optimization code can converge to local or numerical solutions.","maximum likelihood|MLE"],

  ["Graph Theory","Core","Graphs","Vertices, edges and relationships.","A graph consists of vertices connected by edges, with directed/undirected and weighted variants.","Use adjacency lists, matrices or edge lists.","Representation changes memory usage and algorithm complexity without changing the abstract graph.","graph|vertices|edges"],
  ["Graph Theory","Core","Graph Traversal","Visit reachable vertices systematically.","BFS and DFS explore graph structure.","Implement queues for BFS and stacks/recursion for DFS.","Traversal order depends on adjacency representation/order even though reachability is structural.","BFS|DFS|traversal"],
  ["Graph Theory","Core","Trees","Connected acyclic graphs and rooted trees.","A tree with n vertices has n-1 edges and unique simple paths.","Represent with child arrays, parent pointers or adjacency lists.","A mathematical tree has structural properties; code must maintain or validate them.","tree|root|leaf"],
  ["Graph Theory","Core","Binary Trees","Trees where each node has at most two children.","Binary trees support recursive structural definitions.","Use node objects with left/right references.","Pointers/references are implementation details absent from the abstract tree.","binary tree|left|right"],
  ["Graph Theory","Core","Shortest Paths","Minimum-weight path between vertices.","Path length is the sum of edge weights.","Use BFS for unweighted graphs, Dijkstra for nonnegative weights, Bellman-Ford when negative edges are allowed.","The mathematical shortest path depends on the graph model; algorithm choice depends on weight assumptions.","shortest path|Dijkstra|Bellman-Ford"],
  ["Graph Theory","Core","Minimum Spanning Trees","Minimum-total-weight tree connecting all vertices.","MST minimizes total edge weight among spanning trees.","Use Kruskal or Prim.","The abstract optimization problem is exact; implementation must handle ties, disconnected graphs and data structures.","MST|Kruskal|Prim"],
  ["Graph Theory","Advanced","Topological Sorting","Linear ordering of vertices respecting directed edges.","Exists for directed acyclic graphs.","Use Kahn's algorithm or DFS cycle detection.","A topological order is not unique; code should return one valid ordering or report cycles.","topological sort|DAG"],
  ["Graph Theory","Advanced","Network Flow","Maximum flow subject to capacities and conservation.","Flow assigns amounts to edges with capacity constraints.","Implement residual graphs with algorithms such as Edmonds-Karp or Dinic.","Mathematical flow constraints become mutable residual state in code.","max flow|capacity"],
  ["Graph Theory","Advanced","Graph Coloring","Assign colors so adjacent vertices differ.","Coloring minimizes or bounds the number of colors under constraints.","Use greedy, backtracking or specialized algorithms.","Finding minimum coloring is computationally difficult; a coloring algorithm may find a valid but nonminimal result.","graph coloring"],
  ["Graph Theory","Advanced","Bipartite Graphs","Graphs whose vertices split into two sets with no within-set edges.","A graph is bipartite iff it has no odd cycle.","Test with BFS/DFS two-coloring.","The mathematical characterization becomes a finite traversal invariant in code.","bipartite|two-coloring"],

  ["Number Theory","Core","Modular Arithmetic","Arithmetic with remainders modulo n.","a≡b (mod n) when n divides a-b.","Use % for remainder, with normalization for negative values.","JavaScript % is remainder rather than a mathematical modulo operation for negatives, so normalization may be required.","modular arithmetic|congruence"],
  ["Number Theory","Core","Euclidean Algorithm","Efficient GCD computation by repeated remainders.","gcd(a,b)=gcd(b,a mod b).","Use a loop or recursion.","The mathematical recurrence terminates for integers; code must handle zero and sign conventions.","Euclidean algorithm|GCD"],
  ["Number Theory","Core","Extended Euclidean Algorithm","Find coefficients satisfying ax+by=gcd(a,b).","Bezout's identity follows from the extended algorithm.","Return gcd and coefficients using iterative state updates.","The integer coefficients must be stored exactly, often requiring BigInt for large inputs.","extended Euclidean|Bezout"],
  ["Number Theory","Advanced","Modular Inverse","Multiplicative inverse modulo n when gcd(a,n)=1.","a⁻¹ satisfies aa⁻¹≡1 mod n.","Use extended Euclid to compute the inverse.","An inverse exists only under a mathematical condition; code must detect non-invertible inputs.","modular inverse"],
  ["Number Theory","Advanced","Fermat's Little Theorem","For prime p and a not divisible by p, a^(p-1)≡1 mod p.","A theorem about modular powers and primes.","Use fast modular exponentiation to evaluate examples.","Computational verification of examples does not prove the theorem.","Fermat theorem"],
  ["Number Theory","Advanced","Euler's Totient","φ(n) counts integers from 1 to n coprime to n.","Totient has multiplicative formulas based on prime factors.","Factor n and compute φ(n)=n∏(1-1/p).","Efficient factorization becomes the computational bottleneck for large n.","Euler totient|phi"],
  ["Number Theory","Advanced","Chinese Remainder Theorem","Solve compatible systems of congruences with coprime moduli.","A system has a unique solution modulo the product of pairwise coprime moduli.","Construct the solution using modular inverses.","Exact integer arithmetic is essential for large moduli; BigInt may be needed.","CRT|Chinese remainder theorem"],
  ["Number Theory","Advanced","Diophantine Equations","Integer solutions to polynomial equations.","Solutions are restricted to integers rather than arbitrary reals.","Search finite ranges, use number-theoretic algorithms, or exploit known structure.","A finite search cannot establish absence of solutions over all integers unless a bound/theorem justifies it.","Diophantine equations"],

  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Floating-Point Numbers","Finite binary representation of real-number approximations.","Real numbers form a continuous field; floating point stores selected finite approximations.","Use Number or typed floating-point arrays.","0.1+0.2 may not equal 0.3 exactly because binary floating point represents approximations.","floating point|IEEE 754|Number"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Rounding Error","Error caused by representing values with finite precision.","Rounding is the difference between an exact value and its stored approximation.","Inspect tolerances, use stable algorithms and avoid unnecessary cancellation.","Mathematical equality may become approximate equality in code.","rounding error|precision"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Truncation Error","Error from replacing an infinite or exact process with a finite approximation.","Taylor truncation and numerical integration are examples.","Increase steps/order and estimate error.","The algorithm intentionally changes the mathematical process to make it finite.","truncation error"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Conditioning","Sensitivity of a problem's output to input perturbations.","A well-conditioned problem changes predictably; an ill-conditioned one may amplify small errors.","Estimate condition numbers or use stable formulations.","Conditioning is a property of the mathematical problem, while stability is a property of an algorithm.","conditioning"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Numerical Stability","Resistance of an algorithm to error growth.","Stable algorithms control the effect of rounding and perturbations.","Prefer stable decompositions such as QR/SVD when appropriate.","Two algorithms can solve the same mathematical problem but have very different numerical behavior.","stability"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Bisection Method","Root-finding by repeatedly halving an interval containing a sign change.","Continuity guarantees a root under the intermediate value theorem.","Implement midpoint evaluation and interval updates.","The method gives a bracketed approximation, not an exact symbolic root.","bisection|root finding"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Newton-Raphson","Iterative root-finding using tangent-line approximation.","xₙ₊₁=xₙ-f(xₙ)/f'(xₙ).","Iterate until a tolerance or iteration limit is reached.","Convergence is not guaranteed from every starting point and depends on derivatives and function behavior.","Newton method|root finding"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Interpolation","Estimate values between known data points.","Polynomial or piecewise interpolation constructs a function matching given samples.","Implement linear, Lagrange or spline interpolation.","Interpolation exactly matches selected data but can behave poorly outside or between points depending on method.","interpolation|splines"],
  ["Numerical Mathematics","Advanced","Numerical Integration","Approximate definite integrals from finite samples.","Quadrature rules replace a continuous integral with weighted sums.","Implement trapezoid, Simpson or adaptive quadrature.","Accuracy depends on smoothness, step size and floating-point error.","quadrature|trapezoid|Simpson"],

  ["Optimization","Advanced","Objective Functions","Functions to minimize or maximize.","Optimization defines an objective over a feasible domain.","Represent objective as a JavaScript function.","The function is mathematical; code must define evaluation behavior, invalid inputs and numerical domain.","objective function"],
  ["Optimization","Advanced","Local & Global Extrema","Points achieving local or global minima/maxima.","Global extrema compare against the entire feasible domain; local extrema only a neighborhood.","Use derivative tests, exhaustive finite search or numerical optimizers.","Local search can miss global optima; mathematical guarantees require stronger structure.","local minimum|global minimum"],
  ["Optimization","Advanced","Convexity","Property ensuring strong structure of optimization problems.","Convex functions and sets support powerful global optimization guarantees.","Test or assume convexity and use convex optimization methods.","A program cannot infer convexity merely from a few samples.","convexity"],
  ["Optimization","Advanced","Gradient Descent","Iterative optimization along negative gradient.","x←x−η∇f(x).","Compute gradients and update parameters repeatedly.","Step size, scaling, precision and nonconvexity affect convergence.","gradient descent|learning rate"],
  ["Optimization","Advanced","Lagrange Multipliers","Optimize subject to equality constraints.","∇f=λ∇g at constrained stationary points under conditions.","Solve the resulting equations symbolically or numerically.","Constraint qualification and multiple candidate solutions require mathematical validation.","Lagrange multipliers|constraints"],
  ["Optimization","Advanced","Linear Programming","Optimize a linear objective under linear constraints.","Feasible region is a polyhedron and optimum occurs at an extreme point under standard conditions.","Implement simplex/interior-point algorithms or call a solver.","The model is exact; solver tolerances and numerical feasibility affect computed solutions.","linear programming|LP"],
  ["Optimization","Advanced","Dynamic Programming","Optimize or count using overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure.","Recurrences define values of subproblems.","Store results in arrays/maps and compute bottom-up or with memoization.","The mathematical recurrence becomes a finite state table; state definition determines correctness and complexity.","dynamic programming|recurrence"],

  ["Differential Equations","Advanced","Ordinary Differential Equations","Equations involving derivatives of functions of one independent variable.","ODEs define relationships such as y'=f(t,y).","Represent the derivative rule and use a numerical solver.","Continuous solution curves are approximated at discrete time points.","ODE|differential equation"],
  ["Differential Equations","Advanced","Separable ODEs","ODEs whose variables can be separated.","dy/dt=g(t)h(y) can be rearranged and integrated.","Implement symbolic separation for known forms or numerical integration.","Symbolic solving requires algebra; numerical solving handles broader equations approximately.","separable ODE"],
  ["Differential Equations","Advanced","Euler's Method","First-order numerical ODE solver.","y(t+h)≈y(t)+hf(t,y).","Loop over time steps and update y.","Step size controls local/global error and stability.","Euler method|ODE solver"],
  ["Differential Equations","Advanced","Runge-Kutta Methods","Higher-order ODE integration methods.","RK methods combine several slope evaluations per step.","Implement RK4 or adaptive variants.","The continuous equation is discretized; method order does not guarantee accuracy without appropriate step size and smoothness.","Runge Kutta|RK4"],
  ["Differential Equations","Advanced","Systems of ODEs","Several coupled first-order equations.","A vector y satisfies y'=f(t,y).","Represent state as an array and compute vector derivatives.","Array indices encode mathematical coordinates; solver correctness depends on consistent ordering.","ODE system|state vector"],
  ["Differential Equations","Advanced","Partial Differential Equations","Equations involving partial derivatives in multiple variables.","PDEs model fields such as temperature, waves and diffusion.","Discretize space/time with finite difference, finite element or spectral methods.","The continuous PDE becomes a finite algebraic system with numerical stability and boundary conditions.","PDE|finite difference"],
  ["Differential Equations","Advanced","Heat Equation","Diffusion PDE describing temperature-like quantities.","u_t=αu_xx in a basic one-dimensional form.","Use finite-difference time stepping.","Stability imposes step-size restrictions for many explicit schemes.","heat equation|diffusion"],

  ["Real Analysis","Advanced","Sequences & Convergence","Limits of sequences and rigorous convergence criteria.","A sequence converges if terms approach a finite limit.","Approximate terms and monitor differences for experiments.","Finite computation cannot prove convergence of an arbitrary infinite sequence.","sequence|convergence"],
  ["Real Analysis","Advanced","Cauchy Sequences","Sequences whose terms eventually become arbitrarily close to each other.","Completeness of the reals connects Cauchy behavior to convergence.","Numerically test pairwise/neighbor differences for examples.","A finite tolerance test is evidence, not a proof of the Cauchy property.","Cauchy sequence"],
  ["Real Analysis","Advanced","Continuity","Formal epsilon-delta continuity.","A function is continuous when small input changes imply small output changes.","Sample or bound changes for specific functions.","Finite tests cannot prove continuity over a domain.","continuity|epsilon delta"],
  ["Real Analysis","Advanced","Uniform Continuity","Single δ works for all points in a domain.","Uniform continuity strengthens ordinary continuity.","Numerically sample domains or use symbolic bounds for specific functions.","A finite grid cannot establish a global quantified property.","uniform continuity"],
  ["Real Analysis","Advanced","Riemann Integration","Integrability via upper/lower sums or equivalent criteria.","A function is Riemann integrable when its approximating sums converge appropriately.","Numerically approximate with partitions.","Numerical sums are finite approximations and do not settle general integrability proofs.","Riemann integral"],
  ["Real Analysis","Advanced","Metric Spaces","Sets equipped with a distance satisfying metric axioms.","A metric defines distance and induces open balls and convergence.","Represent points and implement a distance function satisfying chosen assumptions.","Code can store a distance function but does not automatically enforce metric axioms.","metric space|distance"],

  ["Complex Analysis","Advanced","Complex Arithmetic","Addition, multiplication, conjugation and modulus in C.","Complex numbers form a field extending the reals.","Represent z as {re,im} and implement operations.","JavaScript lacks a primitive complex type; the representation must preserve algebraic rules.","complex arithmetic|conjugate"],
  ["Complex Analysis","Advanced","Analytic Functions","Complex differentiability and analyticity.","Complex differentiability is much more restrictive than real differentiability.","Approximate complex derivatives or implement symbolic complex functions.","Numerical tests at sample points cannot prove analyticity.","analytic function"],
  ["Complex Analysis","Advanced","Cauchy-Riemann Equations","Conditions relating partial derivatives of real and imaginary parts.","Under suitable regularity conditions, they characterize complex differentiability.","Compute partial derivatives numerically for experiments.","The theorem has hypotheses that numerical checking cannot fully establish.","Cauchy Riemann"],
  ["Complex Analysis","Advanced","Contour Integrals","Integrals along paths in the complex plane.","Integrate complex functions over parameterized curves.","Sample a complex path and use numerical quadrature.","Complex integration requires orientation and parameterization choices in code.","contour integral"],
  ["Complex Analysis","Advanced","Residues","Coefficients of (z-z0)^-1 in Laurent expansions and residue theorem.","Residues convert contour integrals into local information at singularities.","Compute residues symbolically for known forms or numerically with specialized methods.","Numerical residue extraction is sensitive to contour size and precision.","residue|Laurent series"],

  ["Abstract Algebra","Advanced","Groups","Sets with an associative operation, identity and inverses.","Groups encode symmetry and algebraic structure.","Represent elements and an operation function and test axioms for finite groups.","A code object is a group only if its operation actually satisfies the required axioms.","group|identity|inverse|associativity"],
  ["Abstract Algebra","Advanced","Subgroups","Subsets closed under the group operation and inverses.","A subgroup inherits the group operation.","Represent a subset and verify closure/identity/inverses for finite structures.","Finite verification works for finite candidate sets; infinite subgroup proofs need mathematical arguments.","subgroup"],
  ["Abstract Algebra","Advanced","Cyclic Groups","Groups generated by repeated powers/addition of one element.","Every element is a power or multiple of a generator.","Iterate the operation and record generated elements.","Finite iteration can discover a generated subgroup; mathematical cyclicity may require a proof for infinite structures.","cyclic group|generator"],
  ["Abstract Algebra","Advanced","Homomorphisms","Structure-preserving maps between algebraic objects.","A homomorphism preserves the operation.","Implement a function and test operation preservation on finite examples.","Testing finite samples is not proof for arbitrary infinite domains.","homomorphism"],
  ["Abstract Algebra","Advanced","Rings","Sets with addition and multiplication satisfying ring axioms.","Rings generalize integer-like algebraic structures.","Represent elements and two operations; verify axioms for finite examples.","The data structure does not automatically enforce distributivity or other axioms.","ring|addition|multiplication"],
  ["Abstract Algebra","Advanced","Fields","Commutative rings where every nonzero element has a multiplicative inverse.","Fields support addition, subtraction, multiplication and division by nonzero elements.","Implement finite fields with modular arithmetic for suitable prime moduli.","Ordinary JavaScript arithmetic is not automatically arithmetic in a finite field.","field|finite field"],

  ["Set Theory & Topology","Advanced","Cardinality","Size of finite and infinite sets.","Cardinality compares sets through bijections and distinguishes countable from uncountable infinities.","For finite sets use size; infinite cardinality needs mathematical definitions rather than enumeration.","A program can count finite data but cannot enumerate an uncountable set.","cardinality|bijection|infinity"],
  ["Set Theory & Topology","Advanced","Countability","Finite or bijective with natural numbers.","Countable sets can be listed in a sequence, while uncountable sets cannot be put in such a list.","Programs can enumerate examples of countable sets.","Finite execution cannot demonstrate uncountability; diagonal-style proofs are mathematical.","countable|uncountable"],
  ["Set Theory & Topology","Advanced","Open & Closed Sets","Topological definitions based on neighborhoods and complements.","Open sets contain neighborhoods around each point; closed sets contain their limit points under standard settings.","Represent finite topologies explicitly or implement metric-neighborhood tests for concrete spaces.","A JavaScript Set is not automatically a topological space; topology requires additional structure.","topology|open set|closed set"],
  ["Set Theory & Topology","Advanced","Metric Topology","Topology induced by a metric.","Open balls generated by a metric define open sets.","Represent points and distance functions, then approximate neighborhoods.","Finite samples only approximate continuous topological structure.","metric topology"],

  ["Information Theory","Advanced","Entropy","Expected information content of a random variable.","H(X)=-Σp(x)log₂p(x).","Compute probabilities and sum -p*log2(p).","Numerical entropy depends on estimated probabilities and stable handling of zero probabilities.","entropy|information"],
  ["Information Theory","Advanced","Mutual Information","Shared information between random variables.","I(X;Y)=Σp(x,y)log[p(x,y)/(p(x)p(y))].","Compute from a joint probability table or dataset estimates.","Estimated mutual information depends on sampling and discretization choices.","mutual information"],
  ["Information Theory","Advanced","Conditional Entropy","Remaining uncertainty after observing another variable.","H(X|Y)=H(X,Y)-H(Y).","Compute joint and marginal distributions then apply the identity.","The result depends on a probability model or estimated data distribution.","conditional entropy"],
  ["Information Theory","Advanced","Compression","Represent data with fewer bits using redundancy or structure.","Lossless compression preserves information; lossy compression allows controlled distortion.","Implement or use algorithms such as Huffman coding, run-length encoding or transforms.","Mathematical coding models symbols and probabilities; real compression also depends on data structures and file formats.","compression|coding"],

  ["Cryptography","Advanced","Modular Exponentiation","Efficient computation of a^e mod n.","Repeated squaring reduces multiplication count from linear in e to logarithmic in e.","Implement exponentiation by squaring with BigInt.","Exact modular arithmetic is essential; Number can lose integer precision for cryptographic sizes.","modular exponentiation"],
  ["Cryptography","Advanced","RSA Mathematics","Public-key construction based on modular arithmetic and factorization assumptions.","RSA uses large primes, modular exponentiation and Euler-related structure.","Toy implementations can demonstrate key generation and modular powers.","Educational RSA code is not production cryptography; secure implementations require large integers, padding, randomness and vetted libraries.","RSA|public key|modular arithmetic"],
  ["Cryptography","Advanced","Finite Fields","Arithmetic systems with finitely many elements.","Finite fields support field operations and appear in cryptography and coding theory.","Represent elements with modular or polynomial arithmetic.","JavaScript Number arithmetic is not automatically finite-field arithmetic.","finite field|Galois field"],
  ["Cryptography","Advanced","Hashing Mathematics","Mappings designed to produce fixed-size digests with computational properties.","Cryptographic hash functions are functions with collision and preimage resistance goals.","Use vetted hash implementations rather than inventing one.","A mathematical hash definition does not guarantee cryptographic security; security properties depend on the construction and implementation.","hash|collision|digest"],

  ["Financial Mathematics","Core","Simple Interest","Interest proportional to principal, rate and time.","I=Prt and A=P(1+rt) under a simple-interest model.","Implement the formula with explicit units and rates.","Real financial calculations require conventions for day counts, rounding and fees.","simple interest"],
  ["Financial Mathematics","Core","Compound Interest","Growth where interest is periodically added to principal.","A=P(1+r/n)^(nt).","Use exponentiation and explicit compounding frequency.","Rounding and compounding conventions can change practical results.","compound interest"],
  ["Financial Mathematics","Core","Present Value","Discount future cash flows to a reference date.","PV=FV/(1+r)^t under a basic discrete model.","Implement discounting for each cash flow.","Rate conventions, timing and cash-flow dates matter in real calculations.","present value|discounting"],
  ["Financial Mathematics","Core","Annuities","Series of equal periodic cash flows.","Annuity formulas sum discounted or accumulated payments.","Implement finite geometric-series formulas or explicit cash-flow loops.","Payment timing and compounding assumptions must be explicit.","annuity|cash flow"],
  ["Financial Mathematics","Core","Amortization","Break loan payments into interest and principal over time.","An amortization schedule follows a recurrence of balances.","Loop through periods and update balance with interest and payment.","Rounding at each payment can make a computed schedule differ from a symbolic formula.","amortization|loan"],

  ["Game & Simulation Mathematics","Core","Game Coordinates","Positions in 2D/3D coordinate systems.","A point is represented by coordinates relative to a chosen basis.","Store positions as objects/arrays and update components.","Coordinate conventions such as y-up versus y-down are implementation choices.","coordinates|game development"],
  ["Game & Simulation Mathematics","Core","Motion","Position, velocity and acceleration relationships.","Basic kinematics relates position to velocity and acceleration over time.","Use discrete time integration such as p+=v*dt.","Continuous equations become discrete frame updates, introducing integration error.","motion|velocity|acceleration"],
  ["Game & Simulation Mathematics","Core","Collision Detection","Determine whether geometric objects overlap.","Collision predicates depend on shape geometry.","Implement circle-circle, AABB, OBB or polygon intersection algorithms.","Mathematical intersection can be exact; real-time code uses approximations and performance trade-offs.","collision|AABB|intersection"],
  ["Game & Simulation Mathematics","Core","Interpolation","Estimate values between endpoints or samples.","Linear interpolation is (1-t)a+tb.","Implement lerp and clamp t as required.","Interpolation is mathematical; animation systems add timing, easing and finite frame sampling.","lerp|interpolation"],
  ["Game & Simulation Mathematics","Core","Bezier Curves","Parametric polynomial curves controlled by points.","Bezier curves use Bernstein polynomials and recursive interpolation.","Evaluate with the Bernstein formula or de Casteljau algorithm.","A continuous curve is evaluated at finite parameter values for rendering.","Bezier|curve|de Casteljau"],

  ["Tensor Mathematics","Advanced","Tensors","Multilinear objects generalizing scalars, vectors and matrices.","Tensors have components and transformation laws relative to bases.","Represent components with nested arrays or typed tensors.","A multidimensional array is only components; tensor meaning includes how components transform.","tensor|multilinear"],
  ["Tensor Mathematics","Advanced","Tensor Contraction","Sum over matching indices to reduce tensor order.","Contraction generalizes matrix multiplication and trace.","Loop over selected axes and sum products.","Index conventions and dimensions must be explicitly encoded in code.","contraction|Einstein notation"],
  ["Tensor Mathematics","Advanced","Einstein Summation","Implicit summation over repeated indices.","Index notation compresses tensor operations.","Implement explicit loops or use tensor einsum operations.","The notation hides loops and dimensional constraints that code must make explicit.","Einstein summation|indices"],

  ["Applied Mathematics","Advanced","Fourier Transform","Represent signals as frequency components.","The Fourier transform maps a function from time/space to frequency.","Use FFT algorithms on sampled data.","Continuous transforms and discrete FFTs differ by sampling, finite length and normalization conventions.","Fourier transform|FFT|frequency"],
  ["Applied Mathematics","Advanced","Laplace Transform","Transform functions into a complex-frequency domain.","L{f(t)}=∫₀∞e^-st f(t)dt under convergence conditions.","Use symbolic transforms for known functions or numerical approximations.","Transform existence depends on mathematical conditions; numerical transforms depend on sampling and truncation.","Laplace transform"],
  ["Applied Mathematics","Advanced","Control Theory","Feedback, stability and dynamic systems.","Control theory studies how inputs influence system behavior and stability.","Simulate state-space models and controllers such as PID.","Continuous-time control becomes sampled computation and requires attention to sampling and stability.","control theory|PID"],
  ["Applied Mathematics","Advanced","Game Theory","Strategies, payoffs and equilibrium.","Game theory models strategic interaction among rational agents under specified assumptions.","Represent payoff matrices and enumerate strategies for finite games.","A program computes a model's equilibria; the interpretation depends on the model assumptions.","game theory|Nash equilibrium"],
  ["Applied Mathematics","Advanced","Decision Theory","Choices under uncertainty using utilities and probabilities.","Decision theory combines preferences, outcomes and uncertainty.","Compute expected utilities and compare actions under a specified model.","The mathematics formalizes assumptions; code cannot determine whether those assumptions describe a person or system.","decision theory|utility"],

  ["Advanced Foundations","Advanced","Mathematical Modeling","Translate real phenomena into mathematical structures.","A model selects variables, assumptions, equations and parameters.","Implement the model as data, equations, simulation or optimization.","Code executes a model; it does not make the model true or appropriate.","modeling|simulation"],
  ["Advanced Foundations","Advanced","Dimensional Analysis","Track physical units and derive dimensionally consistent relationships.","Quantities carry dimensions such as length, time and mass.","Represent values with units or enforce unit conventions manually.","JavaScript numbers have no built-in mathematical dimensions, so unit errors can silently propagate.","units|dimensional analysis"],
  ["Advanced Foundations","Advanced","Asymptotic Notation","Describe growth rates using O, Ω and Θ.","Asymptotic notation compares functions as input size grows.","Use complexity analysis to describe loops, recursion and data structures.","Big-O is a mathematical classification, not a runtime measurement for one machine run.","Big O|Big Theta|Big Omega"],
  ["Advanced Foundations","Advanced","Error Bounds","Quantify how far an approximation may be from an exact value.","An error bound gives a guaranteed or estimated deviation.","Track absolute/relative errors and stop algorithms when bounds are acceptable.","A stopping tolerance is meaningful only if connected to an error analysis or validated method.","error bound|absolute error|relative error"]
